Major architectural divergence
------------------------------

The maintained system no longer follows the historical upstream architecture as a synchronization
target. Major project-owned divergence includes:

- Windows-only production support with semantic Photoshop execution routed through the UXP companion;
- an embedded durable Photoshop Guard in the same MCP server process, with required-mode mutation
  control and evidence-bound recovery instead of an external controller/daemon route;
- retirement of ExtendScript/COM/VBS production fallbacks and fail-closed UXP routing for maintained
  semantic mutations;
- a project-owned digital-painting control layer, visual review pipeline, method/stage policy,
  document pinning and source/product identity verification gates.

Selective ports and attribution policy
--------------------------------------

Future code or documentation selectively ported from the historical upstream project or another
external source must be reviewed change-by-change rather than merged wholesale. For every non-trivial
port, record enough provenance to identify the source repository and source revision (commit, tag or
pull request), the affected project paths, and any licensing/notice obligations. Prefer adaptation or
independent reimplementation when the external change assumes architecture retired here. When source
text is copied or substantially adapted, preserve all license/copyright notices required by that
source and record the applicable third-party notice explicitly.

